{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>For you, for a remembrance.</b> Up to now it speaks of the Egyptian Passover; from here onward it speaks of the Passover for generations. <b>For you, for a remembrance</b> — now you perform it for its purpose, and for generations you perform it as a remembrance. 'For your generations, an everlasting statute' — Rashi explained: the limitation of 'generations' is two, for it says 'an everlasting statute.' And if you ask: but we say in the chapter 'There are inheritors' that that case is different, for there it is written לדורותיכם, meaning, since it says 'for your generations,' it implies forever — one may answer: had 'an everlasting statute' not been written here, a disputant would have an opening to object as Rashi explained. And the verse there comes and teaches you that everywhere 'for your generations' is stated, it implies forever, even though 'an everlasting statute' is not written there."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:12.202496889Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}